    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="how-to-play" aria-label="How to play Huepaw">
      <h2>How to Play This Color Gradient Game</h2>
      <p>Huepaw takes a smooth color ramp — hue running left to right, brightness running top to bottom — and shuffles every tile out of place. Your job is to put the gradient back together. It is the same core idea as other daily color-sorting games, built here as a browser game you can pick up in one tap.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pick up a tile</strong> — tap or click it once. It lifts and glows so you know it is selected.</li>
        <li><strong>Swap it</strong> — tap any unlocked tile. The two exchange places; tapping the same tile again cancels.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch the lock</strong> — a tile that lands in its home spot locks with a ✓ and cannot be moved again, so a good placement never gets undone by a stray tap.</li>
        <li><strong>Finish the board</strong> — when every tile is home, the gradient runs seamlessly corner to corner and the board clears.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The daily board is built from your local date, so a new one appears at midnight your time. Practice mode offers an unlimited supply of boards in 3×3, 4×4, 5×5 and 6×6.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="tips" aria-label="Tips and strategy">
      <h2>Tips for Solving a Daily Color Puzzle Faster</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Corner first.</strong> The four corners are the most extreme colors — lightest, deepest, and the two hue endpoints. Placing them halves the search space immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Work one row at a time.</strong> Brightness is constant across a row, so all tiles in the top row belong to the same lightness band. Sort the row, then move down.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow the seam.</strong> Two tiles that blend perfectly are probably neighbours in the final layout. Build small correct clusters instead of hunting single tiles.</li>
        <li><strong>Spend hints late.</strong> A hint names two tiles that belong swapped right now, which saves the most time when only a handful of tiles are left.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn labels on in low light.</strong> Numbers give every tile a stable identity, which also helps if you have color-vision deficiency.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose seo-section" id="huedle" aria-label="Huepaw compared with Huedle">
      <h2>Huepaw and Huedle: What Is Different</h2>
      <p><strong>Huedle</strong> (huedle.uk) is an independent daily color-gradient game. Checked on 2026-11-23, it plays across 4×4 to 8×8, picks the grid size for you from that day's board, swaps by dragging, locks a tile once it is correct (with sound), and adds an archive of past boards, leaderboards and an optional Google sign-in. It is free and, per its own privacy page, runs no ads.</p>
      <p>Huepaw is GridPaw's own take on the genre rather than a copy: our own generator and boards, plus a few things Huedle does not offer — you choose 3×3 up to 6×6 in practice mode, every board has a hint, a target peek and a label toggle, and there is nothing to sign up for. Huepaw also keeps an <a href="/huepaw/archive/">archive of past daily boards</a> (rebuilt from the same date-based seed, so each one matches the day it was played) and shows a today-only fastest-time board when you finish. It also keeps a <a href="#hw-ladder">ladder of best times per board size</a> — 3×3 through 6×6 — which Huedle does not have at all.</p>
      <p>Looking for <em>Huedle hints</em> or today's Huedle answer? This page will not solve another site's board for you — it gives you a fresh gradient puzzle of your own, plus the technique that makes any gradient board click: build from the corners inward, keep brightness bands together, and trust the seams.</p>
    </section>

